/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - At a given pressure, water boils at one temperature, the saturation temperature Tsat.
   - Quality x is the mass fraction of vapour: x = mg/(mf + mg). mf is mass of saturated liquid [kg]; mg is mass of saturated vapour [kg]. x = 0 is saturated liquid, x = 1 is saturated vapour, 0 < x < 1 is wet mixture.
   - M stands for v, u, h or s per kg; Mf is the saturated-liquid value; Mg is the saturated-vapour value; x is quality.
   - hfg = hg − hf is latent heat of vaporisation [kJ/kg]; vg − vf is the volume change on vaporisation [m³/kg].
   - Above the saturation temperature at the same pressure the vapour is superheated.
   - The 10 bar values above are those of the common printed tables; the IAPWS-IF97 formulation gives hf = 762.7, hfg = 2014.4 and hg = 2777.1 kJ/kg, within 0.1 %.
   - z is the known value (T, P or h); y is the wanted property; (z₁, y₁) and (z₂, y₂) are the two table points that bracket z.
   - Interpolate only between two rows of the same table region.
   - A property diagram plots two properties so a cycle becomes a path.
   - On a T-s diagram the area under a reversible path is heat.
*/
