/* book Worked example 2: Q: Steam at 1 MPa (10 bar) has h = 2900 kJ/kg. Find its phase, temperature and entropy. Superheated table at 1 MPa: 200 °C, h = 2828.3 kJ/kg, s = 6.6955 kJ/(kg·K); 250 °C, h = 2943.2 kJ/kg, s = 6.9266 kJ/(kg·K). Saturation at 1 MPa: 179.9 °C, hg = 2777.1 kJ/kg.
   1. Phase test: h = 2900 is above hg = 2777.1 kJ/kg at the same pressure, so the steam is superheated, not wet.
   2. Interpolation fraction from the known enthalpy: (2900 − 2828.3)/(2943.2 − 2828.3) = 71.7/114.9 = 0.624.
   3. Temperature: T = 200 + 0.624 × (250 − 200) = 200 + 31.2 = 231.2 °C.
   4. Entropy with the same fraction: s = 6.6955 + 0.624 × (6.9266 − 6.6955) = 6.6955 + 0.1442 = 6.8397 kJ/(kg·K).
   5. Degree of superheat: 231.2 − 179.9 = 51.3 K.
   6. Check against IAPWS-IF97 at 1 MPa and h = 2900 kJ/kg: T = 230.7 °C and s = 6.8424 kJ/(kg·K). Linear interpolation over a 50 K gap is off by 0.5 K and 0.04 %, good enough for an exam answer.
   Answer: Superheated steam at about 231.2 °C (51.3 K of superheat) with s = 6.840 kJ/(kg·K). */
