/* book: lead: The Clausius–Clapeyron equation gives the slope of a saturation curve from latent heat and volume change. It answers a practical question: water boils at 373 K at 1 atm, so what is its vapour pressure at 350 K? One measured (P, T) pair plus ΔH gives another. A plot of ln P against 1/T gives ΔH. Chemical engineers use it for steam, refrigerants, distillation and vacuum drying. */
/* book Figure 4.6.1: The Clausius–Clapeyron line for water, ln P against 1/T with slope −ΔHvap/R = −4889 K, drawn through the normal boiling point. Dots are steam-table pressures from 330 K to 373 K; the line predicts 42.8 kPa at 350 K against the true 41.7 kPa, because ΔHvap grows as the temperature falls. */
