/* book Worked example 2: Q: Ice melts at 273.15 K with ΔHfus = 6.01 kJ/mol. The molar volumes are 19.65 × 10⁻⁶ m³/mol for ice and 18.02 × 10⁻⁶ m³/mol for water. Find the slope of the melting curve, the pressure needed to lower the melting point by 1 K, and the effect of a 70 bar skate blade.
   1. ΔV = Vl − Vs = 18.02 × 10⁻⁶ − 19.65 × 10⁻⁶ = −1.63 × 10⁻⁶ m³/mol: water is denser than ice.
   2. Clapeyron: dP/dT = ΔH/(TΔV) = 6010/(273.15 × (−1.63 × 10⁻⁶)) = −1.350 × 10⁷ Pa/K = −135.0 bar/K.
   3. To lower the melting point by 1 K needs about 135 bar; the reciprocal slope is dT/dP = −1/135.0 = −0.0074 K/bar.
   4. A 70 kg skater (weight 687 N, call it 700 N) on 1 cm² of blade presses with 700/(1 × 10⁻⁴) = 7.0 × 10⁶ Pa = 70 bar.
   5. Melting-point drop: 70/135.0 = 0.52 K. So at −10 °C the ice under the blade stays solid; pressure melting cannot explain skating.
   6. The ideal-vapour form cannot be used here: both phases are condensed, so ΔV is small and no gas law applies.
   Answer: dP/dT = −135.0 bar/K; about 135 bar per kelvin of lowering; a 70 bar blade lowers the melting point by only 0.52 K. */
