/* book exam traps: Using T in °C. The equation needs kelvin; a Celsius value makes 1/T and ΔH/R(1/T2 − 1/T1) the wrong size, so the estimated pressure is badly wrong. | Dropping the minus sign in ln(P2/P1) = −(ΔH/R)(1/T2 − 1/T1). | Using Vl instead of ΔV = Vg − Vl; for vaporisation the gas volume dominates. | Mixing pressure units in P2/P1; the ratio is dimensionless, so P1 and P2 must match. | Taking the slope from P against T; −ΔH/R is the slope of ln P against 1/T. | Believing that ice skating works by pressure melting. Even 70 bar under a skate lowers the melting point by only about 0.5 K; skating at −10 °C works because of friction heating and a thin liquid-like surface layer. */
